of vanilla regression L39:     4. cite29†4.4 Error bounds for the controllable diffusion models L40:   6. cite30†5 Conclusions L41:   7. cite31†References L42:   8. cite32†A Derivations in Section L43:   9. cite33†B Derivations in Section L44:   10. cite34†C Error Bounds for the Doob’s Guidance Estimation L45:     1. cite35†C.1 The regularity of the Doob’s $h$-function L46:     2. cite36†C.2 Oracle inequality of the Doob’s $h$-function matching L47:     3. cite37†C.3 Auxilary lemmas for the oracle inequality L48:     4. cite38†C.4 Convergence rate of the Doob’s guidance estimation L49:   11. cite39†D Error Bounds of the Controllable Diffusion Models L50:     1. cite40†D.1 Error decomposition of the controllable diffusion models L51:     2. cite41†D.2 Convergence rate of the controllable diffusion models L52:   12. cite42†E Auxilary Lemmas L53: cite43†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L54: \nL55: arXiv:2601.06514v1 [stat.ML] 10 Jan 2026\nL56: # Inference-Time Alignment for Diffusion Models via Doob’s Matching\nL57: Jinyuan Chang Affiliation: Joint Laboratory of Data Science and Business Intelligence, Southwestern University of Finance and Economics, Chengdu, Sichuan 611130, China.\nL58: changjinyuan@swufe.edu.cn Affiliation: State Key Laboratory of Mathematical Sciences, Academy of Mathematics and Systems Science, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100190, China    Chenguang Duan Affiliation: Institut für Geometrie und Praktische Mathematik, RWTH Aachen University, Templergraben 55, Aachen 52056, Germany.\nL59: duan@igpm.rwth-aachen.de    Yuling Jiao Affiliation: School of Artificial Intelligence, Wuhan University, Wuhan, Hubei 430072, China.\nL60: yulingjiaomath@whu.edu.cn    Yi Xu Affiliation: School of Mathematics and Statistics, Wuhan University, Wuhan, Hubei 430072, China.\nL61: {yixu.math,zjyang.math}@whu.edu.cn    Jerry Zhijian Yang Affiliation: School of Mathematics and Statistics, Wuhan University, Wuhan, Hubei 430072, China.\nL62: {yixu.math,zjyang.math}@whu.edu.cn\nL63: August 24, 2026\nL64: ###### Abstract\nL65: Inference-time alignment for diffusion models aims to adapt a pre-trained diffusion model toward a target distribution without retraining the base score network, thereby preserving the generative capacity of the base model while enforcing desired properties at the inference time. A central mechanism for achieving such alignment is guidance, which modifies the sampling dynamics through an additional drift term.\nL66: In this work, we introduce Doob’s matching, a novel framework for guidance estimation grounded in Doob’s $h$-transform. Our approach formulates guidance as the gradient of logarithm of an underlying Doob’s $h$-function and employs gradient-penalized regression to simultaneously estimate both the $h$-function and its gradient, resulting in a consistent estimator of the guidance. Theoretically, we establish non-asymptotic convergence rates for the estimated guidance.\nL67: Moreover, we analyze the resulting controllable diffusion processes and prove non-asymptotic convergence guarantees for the generated distributions in the 2-Wasserstein distance.\nL68: Keywords: Controllable generative learning, inference-time alignment, Doob’s $h$-transform, convergence rate\nL69: ## 1  Introduction\nL70: Diffusion models (cite44†Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015 ; cite45†Ho et al., 2020 ; cite46†Song and Ermon, 2019 ; cite47†Song et al., 2021 ) have emerged as powerful generative tools for sampling from data distributions, achieving remarkable success across diverse domains, including text-to-image and text-to-video generation (cite48†Ramesh et al., 2021 ), Bayesian inverse problems (cite49†Chung et al., 2023 ; cite50†Song et al., 2023 ; cite51†Chen et al., 2025 ; cite52†Chang et al., 2025b ), and scientific applications (cite53†Bao et al., 2024 ; cite54†Li et al., 2025 ; cite55†Si and Chen, 2025 ; cite56†Ding et al., 2024b ; cite57†Uehara et al., 2025a ).\nL71: Recent years have witnessed the development of large-scale diffusion models pre-trained on vast datasets. Despite the robust capabilities of these base models in capturing the training distribution, the target distributions of downstream generative tasks rarely align perfectly with this base distribution.\nL72: For example, in conditional generative learning (cite58†Dhariwal and Nichol, 2021 ; cite59†Ho and Salimans, 2021 ), base diffusion models generate samples from a mixture of distributions, whereas downstream tasks require sampling from specific constituent distributions.\nL73: Similarly, in posterior sampling contexts (cite49†Chung et al., 2023 ; cite50†Song et al., 2023 ; cite51†Chen et al., 2025 ; cite52†Chang et al., 2025b ; cite60†Purohit et al., 2025 ; cite61†Martin et al., 2025 ), the base distribution serves as the prior, while the target is the posterior defined by tilting the prior with a measurement likelihood.\nL74: Furthermore, downstream tasks frequently impose additional constraints, such as human preferences or safety considerations (cite62†Domingo-Enrich et al., 2024 ; cite63†Domingo-Enrich et al., 2025 ; cite64†Uehara et al., 2025b ; cite65†Kim et al., 2025 ; cite66†Sabour et al., 2025 ; cite67†Denker et al., 2025 ; cite68†Ren et al., 2025 ), which must be satisfied without compromising the model’s generative quality.\nL75: To bridge the gap between base and target distributions, researchers have proposed numerous alignment methods (cite69†Xu et al., 2023 ; cite70†Lee et al., 2023b ; cite71†Fan et al., 2023 ; cite72†Clark et al., 2024 ; cite62†Domingo-Enrich et al., 2024 ; cite63†Domingo-Enrich et al., 2025 ; cite73†Uehara et al., 2024b ; cite64†Uehara et al., 2025b ). These strategies generally fall into two categories: fine-tuning and inference-time alignment.\nL76: Fine-tuning approaches involve retraining the base score network via supervised learning (cite70†Lee et al., 2023b ), reinforcement learning (cite71†Fan et al., 2023 ; cite74†Black et al., 2024 ; cite72†Clark et al., 2024 ; cite75†Uehara et al., 2024c ), or classifier-free fine-tuning (cite59†Ho and Salimans, 2021 ; cite76†Zhang et al., 2023 ; cite77†Yuan et al., 2023 ). Despite its conceptual simplicity, fine-tuning presents significant limitations.\nL77: First, it often requires a substantial collection of high-quality samples from the target distribution, which may be unavailable in practical scenarios like posterior sampling. Second, the computational cost of retraining score networks can be prohibitive, particularly for large-scale models with billions of parameters (cite64†Uehara et al., 2025b ).\nL78: Third, fine-tuning is vulnerable to over-optimization (cite78†Gao et al., 2023 ; cite79†Rafailov et al., 2024 ; cite65†Kim et al., 2025 ), where the network overfits to limited target samples or preferences, causing it to “forget” the valuable prior information encoded in the base model. This degradation undermines the fundamental advantage of leveraging pre-trained models.\nL79: In contrast, inference-time alignment (cite64†Uehara et al., 2025b ; cite65†Kim et al., 2025 ; cite66†Sabour et al., 2025 ; cite67†Denker et al., 2025 ; cite68†Ren et al., 2025 ; cite80†Pachebat et al., 2025 ) eliminates the need to retrain the base diffusion model. These methods offer substantial computational advantages and preserve the generative capacity of the underlying model.\nL80: The core technique is guidance (cite81†Jiao et al., 2025 ), which incorporates target information by introducing an additional drift term to the base diffusion model.\nL81: Within the framework of Doob’s $h$-transform (cite82†Rogers and Williams, 2000 ; cite83†Särkkä and Solin, 2019 ; cite84†Chewi, 2025 ), the score function for the target tilted distribution decomposes into the sum of the base score and a guidance term, where the guidance is defined as the gradient of the log-Doob’s $h$-function (cite85†Heng et al., 2024 ; cite86†Tang and Xu, 2024 ; cite87†Denker et al., 2024 ; cite67†Denker et al., 2025 ).\nL82: This relationship has also been investigated through the lens of classifier guidance (cite58†Dhariwal and Nichol, 2021 ), stochastic optimal control (cite88†Han et al., 2024 ; cite89†Tang and Zhou, 2025 ), and Bayes’ rule (cite49†Chung et al., 2023 ; cite50†Song et al., 2023 ). The primary challenge lies in accurately estimating this guidance.\nL83: Guidance estimation methods can be categorized into two main approaches: approximation and learning. The approximation approach, exemplified by diffusion posterior sampling (cite49†Chung et al., 2023 ) and loss-guided diffusion (cite50†Song et al., 2023 ), relies on heuristic approximations that often lead to inconsistencies with the underlying mathematical formulation.\nL84: The learning approach (cite58†Dhariwal and Nichol, 2021 ; cite86†Tang and Xu, 2024 ; cite87†Denker et al., 2024 ; cite67†Denker et al., 2025 ) attempts to learn the necessary components by deep neural networks. Classifier guidance (cite58†Dhariwal and Nichol, 2021 ) learns Doob’s $h$-function via a classifier, but this is effective primarily for discrete labels.\nL85: Furthermore, the convergence of the plug-in gradient estimator of the classifier is not guaranteed, potentially undermining guidance reliability (cite90†Mou, 2025 , Section 3.2.2). To mitigate this, cite86†Tang and Xu (2024) estimate Doob’s $h$-function and its gradient using separate neural networks, increasing training complexity. While cite87†Denker et al. (2024) directly learn the guidance, their method requires samples from the target tilted distribution. cite67†Denker et al.\nL86: (2025) attempt to address this data requirement via iterative retraining.\nL87: To address these limitations, we introduce Doob’s $h$-matching, a novel framework for provable guidance estimation in inference-time alignment. We propose a gradient-penalized regression method that simultaneously estimates Doob’s $h$-function and its gradient, yielding a consistent estimator of the guidance.\nL88: When combined with the pre-trained base score, our method enables efficient sampling from the target distribution without the need for computationally expensive fine-tuning or access to target distribution samples.\nL89: ### 1.1  Contributions\nL90: \nL91: Our main contributions are summarized as follows:\nL92: \nL93:   1. (i)\nL94: We introduce Doob’s matching, a novel guidance estimation framework for controllable diffusion models grounded in Doob’s $h$-transform. The Doob $h$-function is estimated via a least-squares regression approach augmented with a gradient penalty, and the gradient of the logarithm of the resulting $h$-function estimator yields an estimator for the Doob’s guidance.\nL95: Additionally, this method is derivative-free, meaning it does not require access to the gradient of the weight function between the target tilted distribution and the base distribution.\nL96:   2. (ii)\nL97: We establish non-asymptotic convergence rates for Doob’s matching, showing that the proposed method guarantees convergence of both the $h$-function estimator and its gradient. These results provide rigorous theoretical guarantees for Doob’s guidance estimation (Theorem cite91†4.3 ). Moreover, we derive non-asymptotic convergence rates for the induced controllable diffusion models, thereby establishing rigorous guarantees for the generated distributions in the $2$-Wasserstein distance (Theorem cite92†4.5 ).\nL98: ### 1.2  Organization\nL99: The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. In Section cite9†2 , we provide a brief introduction to diffusion models. In Section cite14†3 , we propose the stochastic dynamics of controllable diffusion models within the framework of Doob’s $h$-transform, and we present a practical algorithm to simultaneously estimate Doob’s $h$-function and its gradient. Section cite24†4 establishes non-asymptotic error bounds for both the estimation of the $h$-function and the induced controllable diffusion models.\nL100: Finally, concluding remarks are provided in Section cite30†5 . Detailed proofs of theoretical results are deferred to the appendix.\nL101: ## 2  Preliminaries on Diffusion Models\nL102: ### 2.1  Forward and time-reversal process\nL103: \nL104: We consider the diffusion model for a base distribution $p_{0}$. The forward process of the base diffusion model is defined by the Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process:\nL105: \nL106:  | $$\\,\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{X}_{t}=-\\mathbf{X}_{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\sqrt{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{B}_{t},\\quad t\\in(0,T),\\penalty\\ \\mathbf{X}_{0}\\sim p_{0},$$  |  | (2.1)\nL107: where $\\mathbf{B}_{t}$ is a $d$-dimensional standard Brownian motion, and $T>0$ is the terminal time. The transition distribution of the forward process can be expressed as:\nL108: \nL109:  | $$(\\mathbf{X}_{t}|\\mathbf{X}_{0}=\\mathbf{x}_{0})\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\mu_{t}\\mathbf{x}_{0},\\sigma_{t}^{2}\\mathbf{I}_{d}),$$  |  | (2.2)\nL110: where the mean and variance coefficients are given, respectively, as $\\mu_{t}=\\exp(-t)$ and $\\sigma_{t}^{2}=1-\\exp(-2t)$. The forward process (cite93†2.1 ) is commonly referred to as the variance-preserving (VP) SDE (cite47†Song et al., 2021 ) as $\\mu_{t}^{2}+\\sigma_{t}^{2}=1$ for each $t\\in(0,T)$. Denote by $p_{t}$ the marginal density of $\\mathbf{X}_{t}$ for each $t\\in(0,T)$, which satisfies\nL111:  | $$p_{t}(\\mathbf{x}_{t})=\\int\\varphi_{d}(\\mathbf{x}_{t};\\mu_{t}\\mathbf{x}_{0},\\sigma_{t}^{2}\\mathbf{I}_{d})p_{0}(\\mathbf{x}_{0})\\,\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{x}_{0},$$  |  | (2.3)\nL112: \nL113: where $\\varphi_{d}(\\cdot;\\mu_{t}\\mathbf{x}_{0},\\sigma_{t}^{2}\\mathbf{I}_{d})$ denotes the density function of the Gaussian distribution ${\\mathcal{N}}(\\mu_{t}\\mathbf{x}_{0},\\sigma_{t}^{2}\\mathbf{I}_{d})$. The corresponding time-reversal process (cite94†Anderson, 1982 ) of (cite93†2.1 ) is characterized by:\nL114:  | $\\displaystyle\\,\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{X}_{t}^{\\leftarrow}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\Big(\\mathbf{X}_{t}^{\\leftarrow}+2\\overbrace{\\nabla\\log p_{T-t}(\\mathbf{X}_{t}^{\\leftarrow})}^{\\text{base score}}\\Big)\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\sqrt{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{B}_{t},\\quad t\\in(0,T),$  |  | (2.4)\nL115:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{X}_{0}^{\\leftarrow}$  | $\\displaystyle\\sim p_{T},$  |\nL116: \nL117: where the marginal density of $\\mathbf{X}_{t}^{\\leftarrow}$ coincides $p_{T-t}$ for each $t\\in(0,T)$.\nL118: ### 2.2  Path measure and filtration\nL119: We formally define the probability space for the time-reversal process (cite95†2.4 ). Let $\\Omega\\coloneq C([0,T],{\\mathbb{R}}^{d})$ be the space of continuous functions mapping $[0,T]$ to ${\\mathbb{R}}^{d}$, equipped with the topology of uniform convergence. Let ${\\mathcal{F}}$ be the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra on $\\Omega$. We define the canonical process $\\mathbf{X}^{\\leftarrow}$ on $\\Omega$ via the coordinate mapping $\\mathbf{X}_{t}^{\\leftarrow}(\\omega)=\\omega(t)$ for all $\\omega\\in\\Omega$.\nL120: The natural filtration is given by ${\\mathbb{F}}\\coloneq({\\mathcal{F}}_{t})_{0\\leq t\\leq T}$, where ${\\mathcal{F}}_{t}\\coloneq\\sigma(\\mathbf{X}_{s}^{\\leftarrow}\\mid 0\\leq s\\leq t)$ is the $\\sigma$-algebra generated by the path up to time $t$. We denote by ${\\mathbb{P}}$ the probability measure on $(\\Omega,{\\mathcal{F}})$ induced by the law of the solution to the SDE (cite95†2.4 ) with initial distribution $\\mathbf{X}_{0}^{\\leftarrow}\\sim p_{T}$.\nL121: Consequently, the filtered probability space is denoted as $(\\Omega,{\\mathcal{F}},{\\mathbb{F}},{\\mathbb{P}})$, and $\\mathbf{B}_{t}$ is an ${\\mathbb{F}}$-Brownian motion under ${\\mathbb{P}}$.\nL122: ### 2.3  Training phase: base score matching\nL123: In generative learning, the exact base score $\\nabla\\log p_{t}$ in the time-reversal process (cite95†2.4 ) is intractable. One can estimate the base score using samples from the base density $p_{0}$ via standard techniques such as implicit score matching (cite96†Hyvärinen, 2005 ), sliced score matching (cite97†Song et al., 2020 ), and denoising score matching (cite98†Vincent, 2011 ). Let $\\widehat{\\mathbf{s}}:(0,T)\\times{\\mathbb{R}}^{d}\\to{\\mathbb{R}}^{d}$ denote an estimator for the prior score, that is,\nL124:  | $$\\|\\widehat{\\mathbf{s}}(t,\\cdot)-\\nabla\\log p_{T-t}\\|_{L^{2}(p_{T-t})}\\leq\\varepsilon,$$  |  | (2.5)\nL125: for a small tolerence $\\varepsilon\\ll 1$. Considerable research has established theoretical guarantees for this score estimation (cite99†Tang and Yang, 2024 ; cite100†Oko et al., 2023 ; cite101†Fu et al., 2024 ; cite102†Ding et al., 2024a ), leveraging standard techniques from non-parametric regression with deep neural networks (cite103†Bauer and Kohler, 2019 ; cite104†Schmidt-Hieber, 2020 ; cite105†Kohler and Langer, 2021 ; cite106†Jiao et al., 2023 ).\nL126: ### 2.4  Inference phase: sampling\nL127: Given a base score estimator $\\widehat{\\mathbf{s}}$ in (cite107†2.5 ), the inference phase of diffusion models aims to generate samples by simulating the time-reversal process with estimated score. Since the explicit solution of the time-reversal process is intractable, we employ an exponential integrator (cite108†Hochbruck and Ostermann, 2005 ; cite109†Hochbruck and Ostermann, 2010 ; cite110†Lu et al., 2022a ; cite111†Zhang and Chen, 2023 ).\nL128: This approach is well-suited for solving the time-reversal process due to the semi-linearity of the drift term of the SDE in (cite95†2.4 ).\nL129: Let $K\\in{\\mathbb{N}}$ denote the number of discretization steps, and let $T_{0}>0$ be an early-stopping time. We define a sequence of uniform time points $t_{k}\\coloneq kh$ for $k=0,\\dots,K$, where the step size is $h\\coloneq(T-T_{0})/K$. In each time sub-interval, the exponential integrator approximates the score function by its value at the left endpoint:\nL130:  | $\\displaystyle\\,\\mathrm{d}\\widehat{\\mathbf{X}}_{t}^{\\leftarrow}$  | $\\displaystyle=(\\widehat{\\mathbf{X}}_{t}^{\\leftarrow}+2\\widehat{\\mathbf{s}}(kh,\\widehat{\\mathbf{X}}_{kh}^{\\leftarrow}))\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\sqrt{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{B}_{t},\\quad t\\in[kh,(k+1)h),$  |  | (2.6)\nL131:  | $\\displaystyle\\widehat{\\mathbf{X}}_{0}^{\\leftarrow}$  | $\\displaystyle\\sim{\\mathcal{N}}({\\mathbf{0}},\\mathbf{I}_{d}),$  |\nL132: where $0\\leq k\\leq K-1$. The resulting linear approximation to the original semi-linear SDE has the following explicit solution:\nL133: \nL134:  | $$\\widehat{\\mathbf{X}}_{(k+1)h}^{\\leftarrow}=\\exp(h)\\widehat{\\mathbf{X}}_{kh}^{\\leftarrow}+2\\phi^{2}(h)\\widehat{\\mathbf{s}}(kh,\\widehat{\\mathbf{X}}_{kh}^{\\leftarrow})+\\phi(2h)\\bm{\\xi}_{k},\\quad 0\\leq k\\leq K-1,$$  |\nL135: \nL136: where $\\phi(z)\\coloneq\\sqrt{\\exp(z)-1}$, and $\\bm{\\xi}_{0},\\ldots,\\bm{\\xi}_{K-1}$ are i.i.d. standard Gaussian random variables.\nL137: ###### Remark 2.1 (Initialization).\nL138: Note that the true initial distribution of the time-reversal process (cite95†2.4 ) is $p_{T}$, rather than the ${\\mathcal{N}}({\\mathbf{0}},\\mathbf{I}_{d})$ used in (cite112†2.6 ). We adopt the standard normal distribution because sampling from ${\\mathcal{N}}({\\mathbf{0}},\\mathbf{I}_{d})$ is significantly more computationally tractable.\nL139: This approximation is justified by the fact that $p_{T}$ converges to ${\\mathcal{N}}({\\mathbf{0}},\\mathbf{I}_{d})$ exponentially in KL-divergence as $T\\to\\infty$ (cite113†Bakry et al., 2014 ; cite114†Vempala and Wibisono, 2019 ); thus, the Gaussian initialization is valid for a sufficiently large terminal time $T$.\nL140: ## 3  Controllable Diffusion Models via Doob’s Matching\nL141: \nL142: In this section, we propose controllable diffusion models for sampling from a target distribution, defined as the base distribution tilted by a weight function. We utilize the theory of measure change for diffusion processes on the filtered space $(\\Omega,{\\mathcal{F}},{\\mathbb{F}},{\\mathbb{P}})$.\nL143: ### 3.1  Problem setup\nL144: \nL145: We assume access to a pre-trained base diffusion model (cite112†2.6 ) that generates samples approximately from the base distribution $p_{0}$. We aim to sample from a tilted distribution $q_{0}$, defined by reweighting the base distribution with a known weight function $w:{\\mathbb{R}}^{d}\\to{\\mathbb{R}}_{\\geq 0}$:\nL146:  | $$q_{0}(\\mathbf{x})\\coloneq\\frac{w(\\mathbf{x})p_{0}(\\mathbf{x})}{Z},\\quad\\text{where}\\penalty\\ Z\\coloneq\\int w(\\mathbf{x})p_{0}(\\mathbf{x})\\,\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{x}<\\infty.$$  |  | (3.1)\nL147: \nL148: Our goal is to derive a new diffusion process that generates samples from the tilted distribution $q_{0}$ directly by introducing a drift correction to the pre-trained base diffusion model (cite112†2.6 ).\nL149: \nL150: This problem encompasses a wide range of application scenarios.\nL151: ###### Example 1 (Bayesian inverse problems).\nL152: Bayesian inverse problems play a critical role in scientific computing (cite115†Stuart, 2010 ; cite116†Kantas et al., 2014 ; cite56†Ding et al., 2024b ), image science (cite49†Chung et al., 2023 ; cite117†Mardani et al., 2024 ; cite60†Purohit et al., 2025 ; cite52†Chang et al., 2025b ), and medical imaging (cite118†Song et al., 2022 ).\nL153: In Bayesian inverse problems, we aim to recover an unknown signal $\\mathbf{X}_{0}\\in{\\mathbb{R}}^{d}$ from noisy measurements $\\mathbf{Y}\\in{\\mathbb{R}}^{m}$, which are linked by the following measurement model:\nL154:  | $$\\mathbf{Y}={\\mathcal{A}}(\\mathbf{X}_{0})+\\mathbf{n},$$  |  | (3.2)\nL155: where $\\mathcal{A}:{\\mathbb{R}}^{d}\\rightarrow{\\mathbb{R}}^{m}$ is a known measurement operator, and $\\mathbf{n}\\in{\\mathbb{R}}^{m}$ represents a measurement noise with a known distribution. The Bayesian approach incorporates prior knowledge about $\\mathbf{X}_{0}$ in the form of a prior distribution $p_{0}$. Given observed measurements $\\mathbf{Y}=\\mathbf{y}$, the goal of Bayesian inverse problems is to sample from the posterior distribution:\nL156:  | $$q_{0}(\\mathbf{x}):=p_{\\mathbf{X}_{0}|\\mathbf{Y}}(\\mathbf{x}|\\mathbf{y})=\\frac{w(\\mathbf{x})p_{0}(\\mathbf{x})}{Z},$$  |  | (3.3)\nL157: \nL158: where $w(\\mathbf{x})\\coloneq p_{\\mathbf{Y}|\\mathbf{X}_{0}}(\\mathbf{y}|\\mathbf{x})$ is a likelihood determined by the measurement model (cite119†3.2 ), and $Z$ is a partition function to ensure $q_{0}$ is a valid probability density. For example, for a Gaussian noise $\\mathbf{n}\\sim N({\\mathbf{0}},\\sigma^{2}\\mathbf{I}_{d})$, it holds that\nL159:  | $$w(\\mathbf{x})=(2\\pi\\sigma^{2})^{-\\frac{d}{2}}\\exp\\Big(-\\frac{1}{2\\sigma^{2}}\\|\\mathbf{y}-\\mathcal{A}(\\mathbf{x})\\|_{2}^{2}\\Big).$$  |  | (3.4)\nL160: \nL161: In Bayesian inverse problems, one typically has a base diffusion model pre-trained on the prior distribution, and aims to sample from the posterior distribution (cite120†3.3 ) without retraining the base model.\nL162: ###### Example 2 (Reward-guided generation).\nL163: In the reward-guided generation (cite63†Domingo-Enrich et al., 2025 ; cite64†Uehara et al., 2025b ; cite65†Kim et al., 2025 ; cite66†Sabour et al., 2025 ; cite67†Denker et al., 2025 ; cite68†Ren et al., 2025 ), human preferences and constraints can be encoded into a reward function $r:{\\mathbb{R}}^{d}\\rightarrow{\\mathbb{R}}$. For instance, in text-to-image generation, the reward function $r$ quantifies how well the generated data aligns with the input prompt.\nL164: In practice, such reward function can be learned from the human feedback or preference data (cite121†Stiennon et al., 2020 ; cite122†Ouyang et al., 2022 ; cite70†Lee et al., 2023b ). For the sake of simplicity, we assume throughout this work that the reward function has already been given. A naive approach to reward-guided generation is to maximize the expected reward $\\max_{\\pi\\in{\\mathcal{P}}}{\\mathbb{E}}_{\\pi}[r]$, where ${\\mathcal{P}}$ is the set of probability measures on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{d}$.\nL165: However, solely maximizing the expected reward may lead to over-optimization and degenerate solutions (cite65†Kim et al., 2025 ). To mitigate this, entropy regularization (cite123†Uehara et al., 2024a ; cite124†Tang, 2024 ) is incorporated into the objective functional, yielding the following optimization problem:\nL166:  | $$q_{0}\\coloneq\\argmax_{\\pi\\in{\\mathcal{P}}}{\\mathbb{E}}_{\\pi}[r]-\\alpha\\mathrm{KL}(\\pi\\|p_{0}),$$  |  | (3.5)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the Non-decoupling of Supervised Fine-tuning and Reinforcement Learning in Post-training (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.07389v1)\nciteturn26887view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.07389v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 311\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMoE-DisCo: Low Economy Cost Training Mixture-of-Experts Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.06857v1)\nciteturn26887view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.06857v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 349\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMosaic: Unlocking Long-Context Inference for Diffusion LLMs via Global Memory Planning and Dynamic Peak Taming (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.06562v1)\nciteturn26887view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.06562v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 268\n"
